#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115110 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eea927d4-f52d-5583-879f-d70a0f40a1e0" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "46e2f515-2220-585d-927e-7f8a91af7bcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5800" :question/problem "Graph the curve corresponding to the equation $y=\\dfrac{1}{9}(x-3)(x+2)(x+3)$." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a positive cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the $x^3$ term would be positive:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9}({\\color{blue}{x}}-3)({\\color{red}{x}}+2)(x+3) = \\dfrac 1 9\\cdot {\\color{blue}{x}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{x}}\\cdot x +\\cdots = \\dfrac 1 9 x^3+\\cdots\n$$\n\nThe equation is already in factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are $x=3$, $x=-2$, and $x=-3$.\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{1}{9}(0 - 3)(0 + 2)(0 + 3) \\\\\n&= ((-3)(2)(3))/(9) \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-3,-2,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-2$, as follows:\n\n![](images/71/7164fec4ddfa7b68489775b7feef639b61846188fc603186fe115f492dec606d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115111 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115288 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5914ea8b-a7f5-5fe0-8060-34cb47f5d401" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "3723bac3-ba0f-5c81-a59f-cf26836d7671" :question/math-academy-id "e-5801" :question/problem "Given that $x=2$ is a root of $f(x)=x^3-x^2-4x+4$, graph the curve $y=f(x)$." :question/worked-solution "We are told that $x=2$ is a root of $f(x)$, which means that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x)$. So, to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by $(x-2)$:\n\n$$\nx^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}x^{2} & x^{1} & x^{0} \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}- 4\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid - 2 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nWe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (x - 2)(x^{2} + x - 2) \\\\\n&= (x - 2)(x - 1)(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other two roots of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=-2$.\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (0 - 2)(0 - 1)(0 + 2) \\\\\n&= (-2)(-1)(2) \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-2,1,2$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=4$, as follows:\n\n![](images/77/77ab55ab6a105fd075d27e20286312d2c32118b113c7f0517deee9dbf3a16cff.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115113 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38c7027c-8dc4-5d3a-9a96-5d33d3b8c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "11960e10-03d2-57d2-bff1-d5d4285f4e98" :question/math-academy-id "e-5803" :question/problem "Graph the curve corresponding to the equation $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{2}(x+5)(1-x)(x+2)$." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a negative cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the $x^3$ term would be negative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}({\\color{blue}{x}}+5)(1-{\\color{red}{x}})(x+2) = \\dfrac 1 2\\cdot {\\color{blue}{x}}\\cdot (-{\\color{red}{x}})\\cdot x +\\cdots = -\\dfrac 1 2 x^3+\\cdots\n$$\n\nThe equation is given in the factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are $x=-5$, $x=1$, and $x=-2$.\n\nWe now find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{1}{2}(0 + 5)(1 - 0)(0 + 2) \\\\\n&= ((5)(1)(2))/(2) \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-5,-2,1$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=5$, as follows:\n\n![](images/8c/8c64b2285c626881d766811353184d2ecda06b2eaf2694b7d6b51db257115db9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115114 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e03f532e-bf46-5933-baf3-b59e42b7cc67" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "d73c8c81-0fc1-5722-a134-65664b66b3d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5804" :question/problem "Given that $x=-1$ is a root of $f(x)=-x^3+4x^2-x-6$, graph the curve $y=f(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $x=-1$ is a root of $f(x)$, this means that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, and therefore, we can begin factoring $f(x)$ by dividing it by $(x+1)$. We use synthetic division, as follows:\n\n$$\nx^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}x^{2} & x^{1} & x^{0} \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}- 1\\begin{vmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}- 5\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ -1\\end{vmatrix}5 \\mid - 6 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nSo we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (x + 1)(-x^{2} + 5x - 6) \\\\\n&= - (x + 1)(x^{2} - 5x + 6) \\\\\n&= - (x + 1)(x - 2)(x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to zero, we find that the roots of $f(x)$ are $x=-1,2,3$.\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= - (0 + 1)(0 - 2)(0 - 3) \\\\\n&= - (1)(-2)(-3) \\\\\n&=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-1,2,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-6$, as follows:\n\n![](images/ac/acf7dc9cc405f9ae632df5a437d51c4175331ac59b6221ed9f0586c4c6b47446.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eea927d4-f52d-5583-879f-d70a0f40a1e0" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b987328-b96c-54e5-b3ef-a8481771cb8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ca/ca6e63fa7484bb39b42e05b1843d456da7273140a41f1e56698d86dcbb442f13.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113041} {:answer/id #uuid "edaed251-df82-5f8d-91f1-16b4b286bddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0d/0dea0dbddc4ed16a26a917e9e0ae589a78e5fb38b80fc95972ed6bae8fc39098.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113042} {:answer/id #uuid "26debacb-40a4-5f93-a67b-614127eba273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a4/a420da3798b127b1afccdefbc4c45087da673b92ed4465cd0a9f8a245d8dd454.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113043} {:answer/id #uuid "3170dcc9-810e-56c9-8215-3b566022480c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8bc52ee56cc821a2d498ebee872c620a61c527f0967b3cb6de985bd10ffce97a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113044} {:answer/id #uuid "58d0820b-8c13-59c3-b73d-d1d3e708e115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9a/9a138a79ebd4632740ba5c9556a48086d304c5201ab1ffec03e9a23f3074f206.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113045}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ef212097-2d0c-5ec5-a42a-ffbe33b2b0dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113040}] :question/id #uuid "4eaa158c-d63a-514a-919f-df3efa8bad33" :question/math-academy-id "q-28331" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y = f(x)$ where $f(x) = \\frac{1}{2}(x - 1)(x + 2)(x + 3)$?\n\n(*Note: 